Avoid adding new tech exposure until after the earnings reaction settles and volume confirms direction.

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:22.960
The show used to quote accumulative alpha figure across roughly 45 trades, it is gone from the scoreboard and that is deliberate.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:27.660
A number that quietly vanishes is the oldest tell in conformance reporting.

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:34.960
That figure blended trades whose entry and exit prices could not be tied back to the actual sessions the trade was held.

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:39.080
An audit could not reproduce it so it was not the shows to claim.

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:41.880
The exit rule was the deeper problem.

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:47.440
A position used to be closed on whichever session the next pre-market run happened to price it.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:52.100
So a Monday pick was held about five sessions and a Wednesday pick about one.

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:57.280
Per trade performance was measuring the day of the week as much as the quality of the idea.

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:00.780
The hold is now a fixed, published number of sessions.

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:07.520
Some older trades match no market prices at all and they include the best and the worst results on the books.

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:13.360
They stay published as history, flagged and they are never blended into what the show says on air.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:20.560
What replaced it? From August 18th, 2026, every pick is scored under one written rule book.

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:27.040
Entry at the first session open on or after the pick, exit at the stop or at the fixed horizon.

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:31.200
One position, one thousand dollars, no discretionary exits.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:36.520
The rules and the full trade by trade ledger, including the losers and the voided picks,

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:42.440
are published for anyone to check at the Modern Investing Performance page at nerranetwork.com.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:46.840
An invitation to check with no destination is not an invitation.

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:51.200
The honest cost stated plainly, the record is now small.

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:55.640
So for the next several weeks, the alpha number will be based on a handful of trades

00:07:55.640 --> 00:08:00.940
and will not mean much on its own. That is what an honest track record looks like early.

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:06.600
Do not spin it. This is exactly how a listener should audit any track record they are shown.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:11.260
Ask when the record started and whether that date was chosen after the fact,

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:17.000
ask what the exit rule is and whether it was fixed in advance, ask whether losers and

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:22.400
abandoned positions are included, and ask whether the individual trades are published or only the

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.840
summary. A record that cannot answer those four questions is a story.

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:34.940
Portfolio performance simulated $1,000 per trade. Lifetime totals show 54 total trades

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:40.660
at a 54% win rate with 29 wins, 24 losses, and one break even.

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:50.780
Cumulative profit stands at $236.89. This record began August 18th, 2026 under the published rules.

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:56.840
Earlier trades are history and are not blended into it. Two few trades to call an edge,

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:04.620
a scoreboard, not evidence. Average return per trade is plus 0.44%. Best trade plus

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:14.760
20.11%. Worst trade, negative 11.80%. Current streak is one win. Imagine you allocated $1,000

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:20.160
to a data center equipment aim last week after reading about accelerating infrastructure spend.

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:26.860
Your order filled at the open, yet the position size represented 8% of the overall TFSA,

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:31.480
rather than the 2-3% range that keeps single-name drawdowns manageable.

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:37.140
The mechanism that actually determines survival is the interaction between position size and

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:46.500
stop distance. A 7% stop on an 8% allocation creates a 0.56% portfolio hit, while the same stop on

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:53.880
a 2% allocation limits damage to 0.14%. Professionals always calculate the dollar risk first,

00:09:54.380 --> 00:10:00.200
then back solve for shares so that any single stop breach stays inside a preset daily or weekly

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:06.180
loss budget. What most retail investors don't realize is that widening the stop to avoid getting

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:11.140
shaken out simply increases the position size needed to keep dollar risk constant,

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:14.760
which magnifies correlation risk across the rest of the book.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:20.580
The concrete misconception to avoid is treating stop placement as an afterthought once the entry

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:26.560
price is chosen. Fix it by deciding the maximum portfolio loss you will accept on the idea before

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:33.540
you ever click buy. Before we wrap, screen TSX listed industrials trading at least 10% below

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:38.760
their 52-week high, and check whether the most recent quarter showed rising revenue from data

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:43.700
center or infrastructure customers. That's it for today's modern investing techniques.

